{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":514507,"ns":0,"title":"Argumentum ad captandum","extract":"In rhetoric, an argumentum ad captandum, \"for capturing\" the gullibility of the naïve among the listeners or readers, is an unsound, specious argument designed to appeal to the emotions rather than to the mind. It is used to describe \"claptrap or meretricious attempts to catch popular favor or applause.\" \nThe longer form of the term is ad captandum vulgus (Latin, \"to ensnare the vulgar\" or \"to captivate the masses\"); the shorter and longer versions of the phrase are synonymous.","description":"Specious argument: \"captivate the masses\"","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}